// C's and C++'s preprocessor as facts (decision 0327): a file's directives (mode (a)), and a
// translation unit preprocessed under a configuration (mode (b)).
//
// ---- mode (a): a file's directives ----
//
// A rule that reads one of these relations with its path bound asks for that file's directives
// (`c_pp_file_req(path)`), and the runner answers inside the fixpoint, as it answers `file_line`
// (`language::Answerer`; the scan is `rerail_c_pp::directives`). The answer is every directive
// of the file, whatever the configuration: each branch of a conditional is read. Only the relations
// the program declares are made, so a program that reads `c_include` makes no `c_define` rows.
//
// Positions (`start`, `end`, `body_start`, …) are the file's bytes; `line` is from 1. Directive
// `i` is the file's i-th, from 0; the other relations name it by `i`. A `.c` file is read as C, any
// other as C++ (the two read alike but for a raw string and C++'s `::`).

// Every directive: its kind (`define`, `include`, `if`, …, `null` for a `#` alone, `unknown`,
// `line_marker` for GNU's `# 33 "file"`), its bytes and line.
.decl c_directive(path: symbol, i: number, kind: symbol, start: number, end: number, line: number)
// A macro's definition: `fnlike` is 1 for a function-like macro, `params` its parameters joined
// by `,`, `variadic` "" or `standard` (`...`) or `named` (GNU's `args...`, the last parameter).
.decl c_define(path: symbol, i: number, name: symbol, fnlike: number, params: symbol, variadic: symbol, body_start: number, body_end: number)
.decl c_undef(path: symbol, i: number, name: symbol)
// `kind`: `include`, `include_next`, `import`, `embed`; `form`: `quote`, `angle`, or `macro` (tokens
// that make a header name once expanded); `spelled` with its quotes or brackets.
.decl c_include(path: symbol, i: number, kind: symbol, form: symbol, spelled: symbol)
// A conditional's directive (`if`, `ifdef`, `ifndef`, `elif`, `elifdef`, `elifndef`, `else`,
// `endif`) and its expression's bytes (`#ifdef`'s name); the next directive of its chain; and the
// group each directive sits in (its nearest open conditional's directive).
.decl c_cond(path: symbol, i: number, kind: symbol, expr_start: number, expr_end: number)
.decl c_cond_next(path: symbol, i: number, j: number)
.decl c_cond_parent(path: symbol, i: number, p: number)
// A pragma's first two words (`once`; `GCC`, `system_header`; `push_macro`), and its text.
.decl c_pragma(path: symbol, i: number, first: symbol, second: symbol, text: symbol)
// `#line` and GNU's line markers: the number (-1 where macros give it, `file` then the tokens).
.decl c_line(path: symbol, i: number, n: number, file: symbol)
// The text of `#error`, `#warning`, `#ident`, `#sccs`, `#assert`, `#unassert` and an unknown
// directive.
.decl c_text(path: symbol, i: number, text: symbol)
// The include guard, by clang's multiple-include rule: `#ifndef X` (or `#if !defined X`) first,
// its `#endif` last, nothing outside them. `i` is the `#ifndef`.
.decl c_guard(path: symbol, i: number, name: symbol)
// What the file gets wrong: `unmatched`, `after_else`, `unterminated`, `malformed` (by directive),
// and `unclosed_comment` (i = -1).
.decl c_pp_problem(path: symbol, i: number, kind: symbol)
// Answered natively (t33 K3, `language::adapter::c_pp::Directives`).
.answer c_pp_file_req -> c_directive, c_define, c_undef, c_include, c_cond, c_cond_next, c_cond_parent, c_pragma, c_line, c_text, c_guard, c_pp_problem

// ---- mode (b): a translation unit applied ----
//
// Each unit `c_tu(tu, cfg)` names (`c/env.rerail`) is preprocessed under its configuration, asked as
// `c_pp_tu_req(tu, spec)` with the configuration's text, and answered inside the fixpoint
// (`rerail_c_pp::Pp`). Every row is led by `tu` and `spec`; join `c_cfg_spec(cfg, spec)` for the
// configuration's name. Only the relations the program declares are made, but `c_pp_read`, the
// files the pass looked at (found or not), is always made: the run keeps an answer while they are
// unchanged, so an edit to a header asks again for the units that read it, and no others.
//
// `at` is a directive's `#` (mode (a)'s `c_directive` start) or a macro call's name, in the bytes
// of its file. The predefined macros are the file `<built-in>`.
#include "c/env.rerail"
.decl c_pp_tu_req(tu: symbol, spec: symbol)
c_pp_tu_req(tu, s) :- c_tu(tu, c), c_cfg_spec(c, s).
.decl c_pp_read(tu: symbol, spec: symbol, path: symbol)
// `from`'s directive at `at` entered `to`, `depth` files deep (the unit is 0).
.decl c_pp_included(tu: symbol, spec: symbol, from: symbol, at: number, to: symbol, depth: number)
// A header the pass did not find, as spelled once expanded (`form`: `quote`, `angle`).
.decl c_pp_include_wanted(tu: symbol, spec: symbol, from: symbol, at: number, spelled: symbol, form: symbol)
// Whether the group of the conditional directive at `at` was taken (1) or skipped (0).
.decl c_pp_cond_taken(tu: symbol, spec: symbol, path: symbol, at: number, taken: number)
// A byte range of an entered file that survives the conditionals.
.decl c_pp_active(tu: symbol, spec: symbol, path: symbol, start: number, end: number)
// An expansion (`id` from 1): its macro, where the macro was defined, where it was called, and the
// expansion the call came from (0: the file's text).
.decl c_pp_expansion(tu: symbol, spec: symbol, id: number, name: symbol, def_path: symbol, def_at: number, path: symbol, at: number, parent: number)
// `#error`, `#warning` and what the pass refused: `kind` (`error`, `warning`, `not_found`,
// `bad_expr`, `arg_count`, `redefined`, …) and its text.
.decl c_pp_diag(tu: symbol, spec: symbol, path: symbol, at: number, kind: symbol, text: symbol)
// Answered natively (t33 K3, `language::adapter::c_pp::Units`).
.answer c_pp_tu_req -> c_pp_read, c_pp_included, c_pp_include_wanted, c_pp_cond_taken, c_pp_active, c_pp_expansion, c_pp_diag
